[36m(main_task pid=3800193)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Kristian Kristiansen (explorer)") Kristian Kristiansen (explorer) Kristian Kristiansen (16 February 1865 - 30 June 1943) was a Norwegian explorer who participated in the Greenland expedition of 1888 arranged by Fridtjof Nansen. This was the first documented crossing of Greenland. Kristian Kristiansen was born on the Trana farm in Ogndal parish at Steinkjer in Nord-Trøndelag, Norway. He first became acquainted with Otto Sverdrup after Sverdrup's father Ulrik bought the Trana farm, and Sverdrup found Kristiansen at his desk one day asking if he would like to hear about Sverdrup's plans for explorations. This trip he and his companion, Hans Sverdrup, didn't see any polar bears but they met many igluit (Inuits) on their journey. They gave out helpful suggestions to the people there, including sharing matches with them to help them keep warm.
