[36m(main_task pid=3800193)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Michniów") Michniów Michniów is a village in the administrative district of Gmina Suchedniów, within Skarżysko County, Świętokrzyskie Voivodeship, in south-central Poland. It lies approximately south of Suchedniów, south-west of Skarżysko-Kamienna, and north-east of the regional capital Kielce. The village has a population of 440. During World War II, the region of Michniów was occupied by the Germans from September 1939 until January 1945. Under the occupation, it was one of the local centres of the Polish underground resistance movement against the German control. Michniów was home to a bakery owned by Wilhelm Grossman, a man who hid a Jewish family in his basement, risking both his life and his property, during the war. Grossman's family later established the Michniów Congregation, which became an important place of support for the Jewish community of south-central Poland after the war. The village is located near the former Jewish cemeteries: one was destroyed during World War II by German forces, and another was the place where the Nazis buried the remains of victims from the destroyed cemetery in Kazimierz adjacent to the City Hall in Kielce.[1]
